.TH GPIO 1
.SH NAME
gpio \- the Raspberry Pi's pins from the shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpio
.br
.B gpio in
.I pin
.RB [ up | down ]
.br
.B gpio out
.I pin
.RB [ 0 | 1 ]
.br
.B gpio get
.I pin
.br
.B gpio blink
.I pin ms
.br
.B gpio stop
.br
.B gpio selftest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpio
drives the pins through the GPIO server,
.BR gpiod (4)
(port
.BR gpio ).
Pins are BCM numbers (BCM 17 is header pin 11). On a machine without
GPIO (x86-64, QEMU) the server simulates 28 pins.
.PP
Without arguments, it lists every pin: its mode, level and pull.
.TP
.B in
An input, with a pull-up or pull-down if given.
.TP
.B out
An output, and its level.
.TP
.B get
Its level: 0 or 1.
.TP
.B blink
On and off every
.I ms
milliseconds, played by the server.
.TP
.B stop
The pattern stops.
.TP
.B selftest
With nothing wired: every free pin checked (out 1, out 0, pull-up,
pull-down), then put back as it was; a table, PASS or FAIL per pin.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
gpio out 17 1
gpio in 27 up
gpio blink 17 250
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpiod (4),
.BR gfxhat (1)
.SH BUGS
A pin a device uses is refused ("used by a device"), as is writing an
input.
